X_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES` bytes — i.e., **doesn't include sk_seed**. That's likely a bug in the C code (looks intentional in the test)! Reading more carefully, it might actually be intentional - the SPX prf_addr design might call for excluding sk_seed for BLAKE. In any case, we must match.\n\nMy Rust:\n```rust\nbuf[..SPX_N].copy_from_slice(&ctx.pub_seed);\nlet addr_bytes = unsafe { slice::from_raw_parts(addr.as_ptr() as *const u8, 32) };\nbuf[SPX_N..SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES].copy_from_slice(&addr_bytes[..SPX_ADDR_BYTES]);\nbuf[SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES..2 * SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES].copy_from_slice(&ctx.sk_seed);\nblake256_oneshot(&mut outbuf, &buf[..SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES]);\n```\n\nSo I match - hashing only the first N+ADDR bytes. \n\nBut wait - the same issue applies in `wotsx1.rs` and `fors.rs` where I have my own `prf_addr_inner` for blake. Let me check those:\n\nIn my blake `prf_addr_inner` (in wotsx1 and fors):\n```rust\nbuf[..SPX_N].copy_from_slice(&ctx.pub_seed);\nlet addr_bytes = unsafe { core::slice::from_raw_parts(addr.as_ptr() as *const u8, 32) };\nbuf[SPX_N..SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES].copy_from_slice(&addr_bytes[..SPX_ADDR_BYTES]);\nbuf[SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES..].copy_from_slice(&ctx.sk_seed);\nblake256_oneshot(&mut outbuf, &buf[..SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES]);\n```\n\nYes — also hashes only N+ADDR bytes.  Hmm. Let me check if it's the BLAKE update boundary case. Actually let me look at `blake256_update_inner`:\n\n`if left != 0 && (((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) as usize) >= fill {` — wait, in C it's:\n```c\nif( left && ( ((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill ) ) {\n```\n\nIn C `left` is `int`, `((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F)` is a u64 expression, fill is int. If left is zero (== 0 in bool), the condition fails. Looking at my rust: `left != 0`. ✓.\n\nHmm wait there's another C subtlety. `datalen` is `u64`. So `(datalen >> 3) & 0x3F` returns u64. `fill` is int. Comparison would convert fill to u64. So `((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill`. My code: `(((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) as usize) >= fill`. fi