{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_2xmhex7jzcct4riosig5v7kovibn6ifwmjuwepgkz4h5e3y35fla","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Still different. So gen_message_random and hash_message in isolation match (per FFI test), but the driver output differs. The transcript hash for blake uses blakeX_init/update/final which I may also have bugs in.\n\nLooking at the BLAKE transcript code in main.rs:\n```rust\nblake256_update(s, b.as_ptr(), (b.len() as u64) * 8)\n```\n\nThis multiplies by 8 (correct for blake256() which expects bits). But the transcript test `kat_tr_init` uses the blake state directly and update with `sizeof tag - 1` which is bytes:\n\nLooking at C `kat_tr_init` for BLAKE:\n```c\ntypedef blakestateX kat_tr_ctx;\nstatic inline void kat_tr_init(kat_tr_ctx *ctx) {\n  blakeX_init(ctx);\n  static const uint8_t tag[] = \"KAT-TRANSCRIPT-v1-BLAKE\";\n  blakeX_update(ctx, tag, sizeof tag - 1);  // <- pass byte count!\n\n  const uint8_t sep = 0x00;\n  blakeX_update(ctx, &sep, 1);  // <- byte count\n}\n```\n\nSo the C transcript also passes byte counts as datalen (the same C bug). My main.rs has `feed(c, b)` calling `blake256_update(s, b.as_ptr(), (b.len() as u64) * 8)`. THAT'S WRONG — I should pass byte count, not bit count, to match the C bug.\n\nLet me fix main.rs:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_read_input_tokens":366424,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":832,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2bcf60c-a976-41d2-926b-974741aa24ae","uuid":"3e5a8008-083c-4565-a867-7ea56f13aafc"}
